"The grains move in the plane of the picture, a layer one grain thick between two glass plates; rubbing on the front and back plates is left out. In the drum, shaken box, shear box and landslide each grain is a disk (a short cylinder) {#} mm thick, so a grain's weight goes as its area, as in a truly flat layer; with spheres in one flat layer a big bead would weigh more for the room it takes than a small one, and that would sort them by weight as well as size. In the sand picture each grain is a sphere, so that it settles through the liquid as a real sand grain does. Beads overlap slightly when they press together and push apart with a spring and a damper along the line of centres (a soft sphere model, Cundall and Strack {#}). The damper gives a coefficient of restitution of about {#} (set for two of the smallest beads; where a collision falls between time steps moves it by a few hundredths). The springs are much softer than real glass (kn = {#} m g/d for the smallest bead) so the computer can take longer time steps; overlaps stay below a few percent of a diameter, and the flow does not depend on the stiffness at this level. Sideways, a spring of stiffness {#}/{#} kn resists sliding until the force reaches μ Fn, then the beads slide (Coulomb friction); μ of a pair is the mean of the two beads' roughness. Round beads resist rolling with a torque of up to {#} Reff Fn, angular grains with {#} Reff Fn (a pair uses the mean), a common way to model the corners of real sand grains in a model with round ones. Walls are rows of beads glued to the apparatus, so they are rough and have friction {#}. Densities: glass {#}, quartz sand {#}, steel {#}, acrylic {#} kg/m³. Each grain's size is varied at random by at least ±{#} % (the size spread sets more), so equal grains do not lock into a perfect crystal. The time step is a tenth of the duration of a collision between two of the smallest beads; the simulation runs as fast as this device allows, up to real time, and the clock shows simulated time.",
